The study explicitly models dynamic scheduling using real-time information.

We do not have evidence showing:
- how often ServiceNow customers change policies;
- how long policy-selection decisions take;
- how often the wrong policy is selected;
- whether policy selection causes SLA failures;
- whether policy selection causes overtime or customer churn;
- whether dispatchers lack sufficient context;
- whether multiple policies become difficult to distinguish at scale;
- whether policy selection could be safely automated;
- whether customers would pay for such automation.

ServiceNow Schedule Optimization policies are explicitly built from objectives and constraints.
Objectives have weights, and higher-weight objectives receive greater priority.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/field-service-management/field-service-scheduling/create-policies-schedule-optimization.html
The current objectives include, among others:
- maximize task assignment;
- minimize travel;
- maximize high-priority assignments;
- minimize SLA violations;
- minimize overtime;
- maximize preferred-agent assignments;
- maximize existing assignments;
- minimize delayed tasks;
- minimize unassigned tasks.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/field-service-management/hard-soft-constraints.html

ServiceNow's intraday optimization is event-based.
It can respond to:
- cancelled tasks;
- technicians running late;
- technicians calling in sick;
- PTO;
- new high-priority tasks.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/xanadu/field-service-management/field-service-scheduling/optimize-your-schedules-intraday.html
Current documentation also describes batch, intraday, prioritized-event, and on-demand optimization modes.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/field-service-management/field-service-scheduling/viewing-task-assignments-from-so-runs.html

Current ServiceNow documentation shows that policy selection is an explicit administrative/dispatcher function.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/field-service-management/field-service-scheduling/viewing-task-assignments-from-so-runs.html
But explicit human involvement does not prove:
- excessive workload;
- poor decisions;
- lack of context;
- material delays;
- unacceptable cost;
- customer dissatisfaction.

Peer-reviewed research describes real-life multi-skill workforce scheduling and routing problems in field-service operations.
One study describes a real electricity-distribution problem with two competing objectives:
- completing higher-priority tasks earlier;
- minimizing operational costs.
Source:
https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cor.2020.104908
Another peer-reviewed study describes field-service operations involving:
- technician skills;
- capabilities;
- local knowledge;
- cost;
- task priorities;
- consumer satisfaction.
Source:
https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cor.2021.105472

ServiceNow supports objective weights.
Higher-weight objectives receive greater priority.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/field-service-management/hard-soft-constraints.html
Examples of supported objectives include:
- high-priority task assignment;
- SLA violation minimization;
- overtime minimization;
- travel minimization;
- existing assignment maximization;
- delayed-task minimization;
- unassigned-task minimization.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/field-service-management/hard-soft-constraints.html

Dynamic Scheduling automatically optimizes assignments using current conditions and can:
- prioritize tasks;
- unassign previously assigned work for higher-priority tasks;
- consider skills and travel time;
- reassign work when agents take time off.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/field-service-management/field-service-scheduling/Configure-dynamic-scheduling.html

ServiceNow Intraday Optimization can update assignments as conditions change.
Current documentation supports:
- scheduled intraday processing;
- on-demand optimization;
- group/territory qualifiers;
- attribute overrides;
- changing task conditions.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/field-service-management/field-service-scheduling/configure-intraday-optimization.html

ServiceNow supports:
- preferred technicians;
- secondary technicians;
- excluded technicians;
- required technicians.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/field-service-management/work-order-management/assign-preferred-agents-tasks.html
The optimization engine can use these preferences when assigning work.
Source:
https://www.servicenow.com/docs/r/field-service-management/field-service-scheduling/configure-the-policy-to-assign-preferred-technicians-to-tasks.html

We found no reliable ServiceNow customer evidence showing:
- dozens or hundreds of competing field-service policies;
- frequent policy conflicts;
- unmanageable policy maintenance;
- dispatchers unable to determine which policy applies;
- recurring policy-selection errors.

hypotheses.statement:
OPP-006 should be killed if:
1. all observed conflicts are resolved through objective/constraint configuration;
2. optimization engines already solve the trade-offs;
3. the remaining human work is simply approval;
4. the real issue is missing data;
5. the issue is poor process configuration;
6. another control system already owns the decision;
7. no material customer/business consequence exists;
8. no ServiceNow customer evidence demonstrates the residual burden.

We would need evidence of:
MANY CONTEXT VARIABLES
+
MANY POSSIBLE POLICY STATES
↓
POLICY MATRIX BECOMES HARD TO MAINTAIN
↓
DISPATCHER MUST INTERPRET CONTEXT
↓
DECISION IS RECURRING
↓
DECISION IS MATERIAL
Strong evidence would show:
- policy proliferation;
- frequent exceptions;
- contradictory rules;
- manual escalation;
- repeated dispatcher overrides;
- policy-selection errors;
- measurable SLA or cost consequences;
- inability to encode business context cleanly.

